WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.690
- Welcome to the Lifespan Podcast,

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.670
where we discuss the science of aging

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.920
and how to be healthier
at any stage of life.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.610
Hi, I'm David Sinclair.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.530
I'm a professor at Harvard Medical School

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.210
in the Department of Genetics,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.730
and I'm co-director of
the Paul F. Glenn Center

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.970
for Biology of Aging Research.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.320
This podcast is about
why we age, and efforts,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.770
and things you can do in
your daily life to slow,

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.320
stop, and even reverse the aging process.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.890
In the last episode, we
talked about the skin,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.860
the largest organ in the body.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.470
We talked about how to stay looking young

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.520
and feeling good about yourself.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.530
But today we're talking about

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.440
perhaps the most important
organ in the body,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.260
and that's the brain.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.210
I'm joined today, and on all
episodes so far, at least,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.520
by my-
- Wait, so far, at least?

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.440
- Yeah, well, we'll see

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.273
how you're doing today.
- Wait, do you know something

00:00:50.273 --> 00:00:51.106
that I don't?

00:00:51.106 --> 00:00:52.323
- No, just, I'm enjoying this.

00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:53.990
It's going really well.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.340
Matt and I have been together for years.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.730
We wrote the book "Lifespan,"

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.260
and he's also my lovely
co-host of this podcast series.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.210
- Hey.
- Welcome, Matthew.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.600
- I'm really terrified now.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.340
Like we're coming up on episode eight,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.160
and I'm not going to
be here for season two.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.770
- Well, we're going to do
a season two, no doubt.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.710
And I would not do this
without you by my side.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.710
- Ah, can, Rob, can we make
sure that that part of,

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.920
like that I will not do
this without you by my side,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.810
we really want to make sure
that that gets broadcast.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.276
- Though I've been
known to change my mind.

00:01:25.276 --> 00:01:30.276
- [laughs] So so far in this series,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.853
we've spoken about how
aging impacts our bodies.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.930
And even though very clearly,

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.750
there's really actually no separation

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.030
between our bodies and our minds,

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.770
these are integrated systems,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.300
but we've sort of up until this point,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.680
not talked a lot or much
at all about the brain.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.480
And the reason for this is

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.610
because nobody at all wants
to stay healthy in their body

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.700
if they're not also healthy in their mind.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.533
- Absolutely.

00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.120
And we live in a world where
modern medicine, as we call it,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.570
has been very good at keeping

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:03.890
most parts of the body healthy,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.500
the heart, so cardiovascular disease,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.920
cholesterol drugs, heart drugs.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.160
- Increasingly over the years,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.400
we've gotten better and better and better.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.650
- Right, we're living longer,
but we're not living better,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.470
because the brain is still aging
and getting these diseases.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.720
Alzheimer's and other types

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.740
of dementia are becoming more prevalent

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.020
because we're living
longer, but not whole body,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.950
not holistically slowing
down the aging process.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.510
- In fact, so currently,
about 6.2 million Americans

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.590
who are over the age of 65
are living with Alzheimer's.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.570
That's just one of
several forms of dementia.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.363
But because we're living longer,

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.470
that number could grow more
than twofold to 13.8 by 2060

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:46.000
if we don't come up with
some medical interventions

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.405
that can prevent or reverse
Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:49.405 --> 00:02:50.420
- And there's very little,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.910
there's a recent drug that was approved,

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.210
but it barely works.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.080
It makes a minor difference.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.850
So we have to make a breakthrough.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.450
And what we're going
to talk about today is

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.680
a totally new approach
to treating dementia,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.840
and that is boosting the body's defenses

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.830
against not just Alzheimer's,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.370
but against aging itself.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.110
And it's my belief in my labs,

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.220
evidence that if you reverse
the age of the human brain,

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.200
Alzheimer's and other diseases
of the brain will go away,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.400
and you'll even get your
lost memories back again.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.930
But also, it's not just
about Alzheimer is today.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.130
We're talking about other
things that happen in the brain.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.470
We're talking about molecular changes

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.560
that make cells forget
what type of cell they are.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.477
So nerve cells become
more like skin cells.

00:03:27.477 --> 00:03:28.540
And there's another process

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.910
that's important during
aging that we'll touch upon,

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.570
and particularly about its reversibility,

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.350
and that's loss of blood flow.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.970
- And this is really important
because a lot of people,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.830
if you just say Alzheimer's, dementia,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.344
people think that's far away.

00:03:41.344 --> 00:03:44.050
They're going to fix that
before I get to that point.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.690
But all of us, virtually all of us

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.070
go through some amount
of cognitive decline

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.170
in our middle years.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.000
And so this isn't just about preventing

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.630
these things that are way downstream.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.940
This is about making our mental,

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.840
our intellectual lives better right now.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.520
- Well, the biological clock
is ticking all the time.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.270
We're not talking about a
female's biological clock.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.730
If you've listened to previous episodes,

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:08.340
you know we're talking about what's called

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.730
the epigenetic or Horvath clock,

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.730
and this is ticking from conception.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.800
So even when you're
20, 30, 40, and onward,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.650
that clock is ticking away.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:20.190
And what you do in your 20s and 30s

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.330
will impact how healthy you
are in your 70s, 80s, and 90s.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.700
So it's never too early
to listen to this podcast

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.880
and do the kind of things
that we're talking about

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.932
to slow down that clock.

