00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.440
- Anyone who's been following along knows

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.440
that we have presented a case
over the last few episodes

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.450
that aging is largely a result

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:48.400
of X differentiation of cells.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.290
Let's touch on that really quickly,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.970
'cause that's going to
be an important point

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.590
drawing through this episode as well.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.460
- You won't read a lot
of this in textbooks.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:56.560
This is new science.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.558
- You won't even read the
word X differentiation.

00:07:58.558 --> 00:07:59.391
This is something we-

00:07:59.391 --> 00:08:00.950
- That comes out of our work, yeah.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.220
And this podcast, you heard it here first.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.183
The idea, the new idea of aging is

00:08:05.183 --> 00:08:07.610
that it's not just
random stuff going wrong.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:11.100
Is that there's actually a
program that begins at birth.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.470
What happens during birth

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:16.250
and prior to that is that
cells gain an identity.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.540
We start as a stem cell that's fertilized,

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.460
and these cells different identities.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.390
Brain cells, skin cells, liver cells,

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:23.830
everything that makes our body up,

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.320
the many thousands of different types,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.210
tens of thousands actually are given

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.380
their cell type specificity

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.210
by turning on different
genes out of the same genome,

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:35.043
so almost-

00:08:35.043 --> 00:08:35.876
- Through epigenetic signalers.

00:08:35.876 --> 00:08:37.570
- It's called epigenetic.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:40.650
The epigenome is the
regulator of the genome.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:44.210
And the epigenome is not as
easy to describe as the genome.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:47.100
The genome is just a
chemical with four letters,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.840
four chemicals that is the instructions,

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.100
but then there's a computer that reads

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.320
that software called the epigenome.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.800
Or we also use the analogy,
the reader of the compact disc,

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.700
that old device we used to
fit like 20 songs on it,

00:08:58.700 --> 00:08:59.533
was really exciting.

00:08:59.533 --> 00:09:01.410
But what we can now do
instead of 20 songs,

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.038
there's 20,000 genes,

00:09:03.038 --> 00:09:04.450
but the reader is the epigenome.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.950
And over time, by that analogy,

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:09.300
aging is due to scratches on the CD,

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.867
and you cannot read the right
songs at the right time.

00:09:10.867 --> 00:09:13.520
And a brain cell over time
starts to play the music

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.300
of a liver cell or a skin cell
and doesn't function as well.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:17.660
And we get diseases of aging,

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.550
including Alzheimer's as a result.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:21.730
- In the last episode, we said that skin,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:24.050
it appears that skin ages faster

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.640
than most other systems in our body.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.150
And we can use these clocks also,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.480
and have used these clocks to
measure aging in the brain.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.950
And when we measure aging in the brain,

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.770
we find something really interesting.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.350
- That the brain ages slower
than the rest of the body.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.240
- Like significantly slower.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.540
Your brain is biologic,

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.200
very good chance that your brain is

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.250
biologically younger than you are.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.083
- Well, thank goodness,

00:09:45.083 --> 00:09:46.580
'cause it's the most important organ.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.610
Without a functioning brain,
we're really quite useless.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.180
- So one of these studies was led,

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:53.700
actually a couple of these studies was led

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:57.150
by your friend and sometimes
collaborator Steve Horvath.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.140
- All right, before I get
into Steve Horvath's work

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.720
on the cerebellum, important stuff,

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.000
let's talk about how that
clock is actually measured.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.080
'Cause that'll be important later

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.470
for when we talk about diet.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:09.210
Steve measures the methylation on the DNA.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.880
It's a chemical called a methyl

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.730
that the cells add to the
DNA and it sticks there,

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:14.800
it doesn't wash away,

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:16.850
and it makes sure cells
have their identity

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.910
and play the right genes
for the rest of your life.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:20.770
The problem is with aging, that changes,

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:22.100
and you can measure that.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.070
In my lab, we can do
it for about a dollar.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:27.280
And then the readout tells
you, okay, that brain,

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.157
even though the person is 40
years old, actually is 50.

00:10:30.157 --> 00:10:31.460
- But you could look at the liver

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.177
and you could see that
the liver is actually 55,

00:10:33.177 --> 00:10:34.750
and you could look at the skin and say,

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:38.850
oh, the liver's actually
62 in equivalent numbers.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:42.280
Horvath and his associates looked

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.800
at tissue samples from 112-year-old woman.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:47.490
And they measured the clock

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.410
in all these different parts of her body.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:53.640
And the regions of the
brain were all much,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.860
much less methylated
than her other organs.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:56.820
So that was the first study.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.610
And then they've done other
studies just a few years later.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.990
They did a study that showed
across a lot of samples,

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.670
predicted age is lower in the brain

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.630
than really anywhere else in the body.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.500
Sometimes like a quarter of the age

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:13.840
of other parts of the body.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:14.850
- Well, there's a practical

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.580
and an evolutionary explanation for those.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:19.020
The practical one is that
our brain is protected.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.090
There's a blood-brain barrier.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:21.533
It doesn't get hit by UV
light like our skin does.

00:11:21.533 --> 00:11:23.650
- We have these big thick skulls.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:26.300
- We're basically lollipops on a stick.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.620
But our brain needs to be protected

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.330
from these toxins that
are in the environment,

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.060
and of course anything
that leads to brain aging.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.430
But of course, our brains will still age.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.090
DNA breaks, we've talked about.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.350
Broken chromosomes accelerate that clock.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:41.470
And this happens naturally even
to cells that don't divide,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.840
including neurons, the
nerve cells in our brain.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.730
- One thing to note is

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:49.790
that a cellular turnover is
relatively low in the brain.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.620
So even though these cells,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:54.940
even if they're not
dividing, they still do age.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.330
That process of slow cellular turnover

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.960
could explain some of the reasons

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:00.670
why the brain doesn't X differentiate

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:02.620
as fast as other regions in the body.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.050
- Yeah, that's probably right.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:06.320
Unlike the liver, you can cut
a piece out, it grows back,

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:07.683
the brain doesn't easily do that.

00:12:07.683 --> 00:12:10.560
There's a little
neurogenesis, as we call it,

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.160
but mostly those nerve cells are

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:13.600
going to there for your whole life.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.410
And so they have these
super protective mechanisms,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.360
these adversity systems

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.620
that keep the brain younger for longer,

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.070
but they're not perfect, of course.

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:23.870
We do have an aging brain,

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:26.360
but there are ways to
turn on those defenses

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:29.430
greater than they naturally
would be activated.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.180
- And so all of this is the good news.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:32.750
The good news is, in general,

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:36.100
our brains age more slowly
than the rest of our bodies.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:38.410
The bad news, as we said at
the beginning of the show,

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:39.970
is that we are living longer.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.120
That's actually good news too,

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:42.340
but it's got a bad news component

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.590
because our bodies are now outliving,

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:46.840
in some cases, our brain.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:49.210
The average onset of
dementia is 80 years old.

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:52.550
And in most advanced nations now,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.780
the average lifespan is
more than 80 years old.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:57.960
And so what that really means is

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:02.940
that more and more of us
are living with dementia

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:07.550
at the ends of our lives and
other cognitive impairments.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.530
That's a terrible fate.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:13.260
That's something that we should
all be working to prevent.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:14.980
- Well, that's why I work on aging

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:18.840
and not Alzheimer's disease
or cancer, specifically.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.160
As we've said in earlier episodes,

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:24.030
by working on aging, we can
keep the whole body young,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.950
including the brain, which
is really what we want to do

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:29.860
if we want to have maximal
gains in longevity and health

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.770
and mental capacity right up to the end.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:33.680
- And again, there's not,

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.560
because you work on the whole body,

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.270
you're not working on the whole
body when people become old.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:39.320
We're talking about the whole body

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.350
across the span of a human life.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.180
And there are advantages to that too,

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:47.310
because everybody suffers
some cognitive decline.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:48.930
This is a little terrifying.

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.110
The volume of the brain

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:56.110
after the age of 40 reduces
about 5% per decade.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.790
And that's got cognitive implications

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.250
for all of us as we enter

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:04.440
what's used to be called our middle years.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:05.310
- Well, yeah, I'm 52.

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:07.730
I can already feel it happening,

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.350
but I'm doing certain
things in my lifestyle.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:11.390
We're going to tell people
about some of those things

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.190
you can do to slow that process down

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:14.760
and even reverse it later in life.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.300
- So a lot of people
might think, well, okay,

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.210
it does make a lot of evolutionary sense

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:24.210
that the brain starts to
have struggles at 30, 40, 50,

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:27.540
but for one thing, for a
long period of our history,

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:31.460
people didn't actually
last that long in general.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:34.060
- Right, most males
would die from predation

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.820
or war or starvation in their 40s and 50s.

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:38.790
Some people did make it to their 80s.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:39.810
But for most of our history,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.280
we didn't need our brains as
an 80-year-old, same for women.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:44.730
- Well, and we didn't need

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.230
a really super sophisticated
executive functioning

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.530
to survive even when we
were in our 30s and 50s,

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.540
well, 30s, 40s, and 50s.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.060
If you made it that far,

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.300
your life was going to
be pretty much the same

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:58.120
on Monday as it was on Sunday.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.830
And it was going to be pretty
much the same on Sunday

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:01.780
as it was two Sundays before

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.390
and two months before
and two years before,

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.490
and even like 200 years before.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.470
- Right, as we talked
about in the last episode,

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.500
getting gray, losing your hair was

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:12.000
signs of wisdom and you got respect.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.030
And presumably, you didn't
need to be that quick witted.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:15.020
You had a lot of wisdom

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:16.950
and experience that would compensate.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.940
But we live in a world
now where every year,

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.120
you have to be learning something new,

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.190
and jobs are turning over.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.130
And it all started in the 1700s.

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:26.500
Now we find ourselves at the point

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.770
where it's really difficult,

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.240
even for young people, to
keep up with this change.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:32.900
- And this is what we call
a plastic environment,

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.940
an ever changing environment.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.870
And that has implications

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:38.800
in terms of how our brains develop,

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:41.670
and the plasticity that we need

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:44.090
to respond to these changes over time.

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:45.860
- Yeah, I watched my
grandmother, who passed away,

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:47.070
and we wrote about in "Lifespan."

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.090
She was a mentor to me.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:50.670
Actually the reason
that I do this research.

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.810
She told me to make the
world a better place.

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.830
So she has a very dear spot in my heart.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.110
What I saw happen to her

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.080
as she got older was she just shut down.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.980
She didn't care about life,

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:03.140
but she also didn't care about change.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:05.590
And so she didn't worry about computers.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:07.300
She didn't bother
learning how to use that.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.810
She didn't bother learning
how to play a compact disc.

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:13.870
And the last 20 years of
her life weren't that great.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:14.780
Nowadays,

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.730
you cannot be an older
person and ignore technology,

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:18.760
or you'll be isolated.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.377
You can't talk to your grandkids.

00:16:20.377 --> 00:16:22.700
This COVID-19 and the pandemic.

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:23.907
- Yeah, imagine what,

00:16:23.907 --> 00:16:25.520
and this did happen to a lot of people,

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.280
but this is a really terrible thing.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.180
If you were not able or willing

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:33.063
to adopt new changes to your environment,

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.950
you were not going to be
able to talk to people

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.620
during the shutdowns, during the pandemic.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:40.540
- Yeah, I was just at
the Apple store getting

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:43.730
a new phone just a couple of days ago.

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:45.320
And I was there, there
was a lot of young people,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:47.340
but then in Cayman, an older person,

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.487
she was probably 85, the way she looked,

00:16:49.487 --> 00:16:52.160
and the way she wasn't walking that well.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.450
But she went up, and
I reckon 20 years ago,

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:56.850
it would be rare to see someone

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:58.580
in a computer shop of that age.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:16:59.557
Now she went up and said,

00:16:59.557 --> 00:17:01.370
"Hey, I know about the
new operating system.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:02.410
I need to get a new iPhone.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:05.130
And by the way, my iPad
needs an update as well."

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:06.630
I was shocked, and I
remembered my grandma-

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.660
- Can I link this to my smart walker?

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:09.630
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.780
And I find also in our generation,

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.080
so you're on your 40s, I'm in my 50s,

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:16.220
we cannot slow down either.

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:17.960
We can't say, all right,
I'm just going to deal

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.350
with my compact disc or record collection.

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:22.960
We have to be, use Spotify
to talk to our kids,

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:25.600
we share files, social media.

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:28.070
If you need dates, you
need to figure out that.

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:30.940
Jobs, the job market's changing totally.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:32.760
We used to have one or
two jobs in our lives.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.190
Now we might have three
jobs, we're mobile,

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:38.140
we work remotely, and it's
turning over all the time.

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:41.330
That requires a brain that
is highly, highly adaptable.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.440
- And so what we want to talk about now is

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.450
this idea of keeping our brains

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.600
and bodies aging at about the same rate

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.520
so that we don't suffer physical decline,

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:55.640
and we don't suffer cognitive
decline through the,

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.780
as much of the entirety of
our lifespan as possible.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:17:59.810
And that's really,

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:01.410
as we've had all of these advances

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.946
in keeping our bodies
younger and healthier,

00:18:04.946 --> 00:18:07.130
it's really about keeping the brain

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.100
younger and healthier too.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.843
That comes down to some of the same things

00:18:11.843 --> 00:18:14.310
that we've been talking about
throughout this podcast,

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:17.120
these three longevity pathways.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.800
- Yeah, so those who've
been with us will know

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:23.030
that there are three main
buckets of longevity factors

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:25.170
that respond to adversity.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.660
And these have evolved
to sense the environment

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:28.493
when times are tough.

00:18:28.493 --> 00:18:30.190
We call them adversity mimetics

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.840
that turn on these three things.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.070
So the buckets are mTOR,

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.870
which respond to low
amounts of amino acids,

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:38.170
particularly leucine, isoleucine, valine.

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:39.750
There's the AMPK pathway.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:43.200
AMPK senses low energy,
low glucose levels,

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.480
and makes more energy, more
mitochondria, boost NAD.

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:47.850
And that's important for the third group,

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.220
which are the sirtuins that require NAD

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.010
and can be activated by certain chemicals.

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:55.520
Importantly, they all work together.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:59.430
We'll talk about later that
sirtuins can activate AMPK.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:02.180
AMPK can activate sirtuins and mTOR.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.440
What they do downstream is complicated.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:07.130
We're not going to get into that
except relevant to the brain,

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:08.180
but they protect the body.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.410
They turn up metabolism, they burn fat,

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.230
they repair DNA, they
clear out senescent cells,

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.600
they lower inflammation
among many other things.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.670
- Can we dive a little into mTOR?

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.860
We're going to talk mostly about sirtuins,

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:25.850
but there's a real key role
that mTOR plays in autophagy.

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:26.683
- Well it does.

00:19:26.683 --> 00:19:27.830
They all play a role in autophagy,

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:29.570
but mTOR is the most potent one.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.860
mTOR responds to insulin
signaling and fasting.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:33.693
And in response,

00:19:33.693 --> 00:19:36.230
it mobilizes proteins to be recycled

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.830
and made into new proteins
when you're hungry.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.220
And that's called autophagy,

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:41.480
the garbage collection

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:43.790
and trash recycling system of the cell.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.570
And that's particularly
important for the brain.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:46.710
'Cause as we get older,

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:48.650
there are these misfolded proteins.

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:50.480
In the eye, they're called lipofuscin,

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:51.720
and then in Alzheimer's,

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.510
they're plaques and tangles of proteins.

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:55.710
And to get rid of those, you
need really deep cleansing.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:57.450
It's called chaperone-mediated autophagy.

00:19:57.450 --> 00:20:00.940
And mTOR is a really
great way, not activating,

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.300
but downregulating mTOR,

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:05.390
turns on those recycling
pathways really effectively.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:07.400
We're not going to talk
so much about that,

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:08.630
though we are going to talk a little bit

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:10.870
about changing our diet to ensure

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:13.290
that all three of these
pathways are working

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.240
in the way that will promote brain health.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:17.490
- And even though we're really
mostly going to be focusing

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:19.640
today on the sirtuin pathways,

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:23.440
you could be assured
that the lifestyle advice

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:25.750
that comes along with this,

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:27.400
it works for all three of these pathways.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:28.939
- It does, 'cause they're
talking to each other.

00:20:28.939 --> 00:20:31.630
The reason we're going to
focus on the sirtuins is

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:33.810
in part 'cause I've been
working on them in my lab,

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.970
but also because they become
central to brain health.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:38.640
And so we'll cover both.

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.820
And also what's important
about the sirtuins

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:42.760
as opposed to these other two buckets is

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:45.690
that the sirtuins respond to whole variety

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:47.960
of environmental pseudo-stresses.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:50.460
We call them adversity mimetics.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:51.840
We're going to talk about food,

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.360
talk about exercise and
supplements that you can take

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:58.720
that should activate these three
pathways quite effectively.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.510
And we think based on
animal studies as well,

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.460
and human studies, should
slow down brain aging

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:05.027
and even potentially reverse it.

00:21:05.027 --> 00:21:06.770
- And there's several types of sirtuins,

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:07.960
but one in particular,

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.390
there's a couple that are
important to brain aging.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:13.070
One in particular is called SIRT1.

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:14.140
- Right, so there are seven

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:16.040
of these genes in every cell in our body.

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:19.010
The brain makes a lot of SIRT6 and SIRT1.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:22.340
And over time, the levels
of both go down with aging,

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:26.540
as well as the fuel that
those enzymes need called NAD.

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:28.160
But we'll focus on SIRT1 mostly

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:29.480
because that's what seems to be

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.250
the most important for
controlling brain aging.

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.010
- Let's talk about the role of sirtuins

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:37.900
when it comes to aging in the brain.

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:40.110
- Well, remember this
clock is ticking away

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:41.700
because the loops and the bundles

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.650
of the DNA are getting messed up.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.510
The bundles become loops, the
loops are becoming bundles.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.810
We've linked the sirtuins to that process.

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:52.668
What happens when a cell
is overstressed, overbroke,

00:21:52.668 --> 00:21:55.290
and overdamaged is that
the sirtuins have two jobs.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:58.000
They have to create these bundles of DNA

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.050
and make sure the cell has its identity,

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.720
so the genes are read
like a proper compact disc

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:04.500
or a software in a computer.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:08.320
But when chromosomes
break or you crush a cell,

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:09.640
there's a panic attack.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.500
And the sirtuins rush away to help

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:15.150
with that stress and
repair the broken DNA.

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:16.160
But then they have to find

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:17.330
their way back to where they came from

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:20.530
and reestablish that
structure of the epigenome.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:21.990
And they do a pretty good job.

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:25.520
99.9% of all of those structures
go back to how they were.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:29.640
But that .1% never goes back,
and over time accumulates.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.800
And these are the
scratches that cause aging.

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:35.020
- And in order to mitigate that,

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:37.973
what we want to do is
upregulate the sirtuins.

00:22:37.973 --> 00:22:41.400
And we can do that through NAD boosters.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.670
- Right, well, in the mouse in 2008,

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:46.700
we published that just
upregulating SIRT1 gene

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:49.190
in the nerve cells of a mouse's brain

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:51.210
were sufficient to slow aging and prevent

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:53.750
those loops and bundles from changing.

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:54.810
And that was the first evidence

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.090
you could slow down a mouse's brain age.

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:01.220
But you can't modify our
brains genetically just yet,

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:04.870
so we need to find safe ways
you could take a chemical,

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:07.790
a pill hopefully, rather
than an injection,

00:23:07.790 --> 00:23:10.615
that will increase SIRT1's activity.

00:23:10.615 --> 00:23:13.850
The ones we know of are
resveratrol and NAD boosters.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:15.350
We'll talk about those in a second.

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:20.350
And then they give the benefit
of a good diet and exercise.

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:23.390
And in combination, even better.

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:25.940
- Well, let's talk about
those things first.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:27.430
Let's talk about diet and exercise,

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.220
'cause that's got to be the foundation

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:33.030
on which we build all this other stuff.

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:33.863
- Well, absolutely.

00:23:33.863 --> 00:23:36.510
We've talked previously
about the Mediterranean diet.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.010
And one of the reasons
is that it's very clear

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:42.840
in over a dozen studies that
a Mediterranean-type diet

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:44.130
protects the brain from aging,

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.600
and can even reverse aspects of aging

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:49.450
in the elderly with mild
cognitive impairments.

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.560
- Yeah, there's a study earlier this year

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:53.590
from a really large
international research group.

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:56.210
It was led by Tommaso Boldrini.

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:58.430
It showed higher adherence
to a healthy diet,

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:00.640
in this case, the Mediterranean diet,

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:02.980
is associated with less amyloid beta,

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:05.830
less tau, larger gray matter volume.

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.650
If some of these words
are sounding familiar,

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:08.900
it's because they're all associated

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:10.230
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.980
- And there was a second study
that I found fascinating.

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:16.410
This one was by Anastasio et al., 2017.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:19.230
And it was over 1,000,
actually close to 2000 people.

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:22.130
And there was a 10%
reduction in dementia risk

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:24.720
for people on the Mediterranean diet.

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:25.553
And-

00:24:25.553 --> 00:24:28.010
- There's actually a 10% risk reduction

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:29.980
for each Mediterranean diet score,

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:33.390
which means the more
Mediterranean your diet,

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.010
the better you were doing.

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:37.320
- Right, and so that includes
olive oil in the diet,

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:40.910
red wine, and not a lot of red meat.

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:42.400
- Yeah, and not surprisingly,

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:44.240
this is a research team from Greece.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:46.160
They really like the Mediterranean diet

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:47.740
for obvious reasons.

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:50.000
- Yeah, what I want to get into later,

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:51.370
and hopefully we will is,

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:55.010
what is it in that diet chemically
that can help the brain?

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:56.170
- Well, I don't know that we need

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:57.490
to wait too long to get into that.

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:58.410
Let's talk about that.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:01.830
What is it in that diet chemically
that can help the brain?

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:03.090
- Well, there are a number of things.

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:04.890
I would put them into a
few different buckets.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:08.210
There are vitamin that can be deficient,

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:10.830
that we need to talk
about the clock later.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:14.020
But first up, there's red
wine, which has polyphenols.

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:15.137
Resveratrol is one of those.

00:25:15.137 --> 00:25:18.590
And my lab has been working
on resveratrol for many years.

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:20.320
And that directly activates SIRT1.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:22.970
It makes the enzyme, it's like Pac-Man,

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:25.380
and it's controlling
genes and it works faster.

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:27.700
So resveratrol from red wine

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:29.340
has clearly been shown to be beneficial,

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:30.460
and also prevents cancer

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:33.870
and not just has metabolic
and brain-enhancing effects.

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:36.030
The other component of Mediterranean diet

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.610
that works on SIRT1 is olive oil.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:42.300
And [indistinct] recently showed
that if you add oleic acid,

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.860
which is a major component of olive oil,

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:47.260
it's also found in avocados
and other good foods like that,

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.770
can also directly activate the enzyme

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:52.350
by sticking to it and making this Pac-Man,

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:53.940
I don't know if everyone
knows what a Pac-Man is,

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.860
this little puppet creature
on an electronic game,

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:57.950
chomp faster.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.230
And there's probably other molecules.

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:01.780
We talked previously in another episode

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:03.800
about xenohormetic molecules.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.980
Plants make these molecules
to survive stress,

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:07.100
and when we eat them,

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:09.820
we get the benefits of that
stress, because we worry,

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:12.280
our bodies worry that our
food supply might run out.

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:14.700
- One of the, and that's sort
of the overarching nature

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:15.730
of a plant-based diet,

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.350
whether it's Mediterranean
diet or some other diet,

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:21.500
is that it is mimicking adversity.

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:23.610
Your body's got to work a little harder

00:26:23.610 --> 00:26:25.800
to get everything that it needs,

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.250
that's sending the signals that
maybe times aren't so great

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:31.280
and we need to activate
these longevity pathways.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:33.770
- Well, that's the difference
between a Mediterranean diet,

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:38.360
and a high fat carnivorous diet,
and a typical Western diet.

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:39.540
They're full with calories,

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.330
full with a whole bunch of stuff

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:43.110
that tells the body times are good.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:44.180
It's a bounty.

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:45.810
No need to protect ourselves.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:47.550
Let's just burn the candle at both ends

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:49.680
and forget about life later.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:50.860
And that's not what you want.

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:53.720
What you want to do is to have
the perception of adversity.

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:56.570
And the Mediterranean
diet, as well as in Japan,

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:57.800
what's called the Okinawan diet,

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:02.240
which has low levels of
protein and mostly plant-based,

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.940
those two trick the body into thinking

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:09.012
that the food supply sucks
and could run out any minute.

00:27:09.012 --> 00:27:12.580
- And these diets aren't perfect, right?

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:13.413
Let's talk about some of the things

00:27:13.413 --> 00:27:16.100
that you need to make sure
that you're getting enough of

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:19.240
if you are eating this plant-based diet.

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:21.470
- Right, well, one of the
first things to worry about

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:24.200
if you're just focusing on plants only is

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.840
a deficiency in folic acid.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:28.660
This is vitamin B12.

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:30.170
Low-
- This is the supplementation

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:31.560
that they give to pregnant women,

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:33.090
'cause they're often low in folic acid.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:34.500
- Yeah, and there's a good reason why,

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:35.870
which I'll get to in a second.

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:37.830
There are other B vitamins
that are also important

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:39.210
to make sure that you have enough of,

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:40.730
vitamin B6 and B3.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.110
We've talked about B3 as early component

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:45.740
that's building up that
NAD for the sirtuins.

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:48.050
So B vitamins, especially if
you're on a plant-based diet,

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.970
but for everybody, don't
be deficient in these.

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:51.803
Why?

00:27:51.803 --> 00:27:53.680
Because the B vitamins are the ones

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.100
that make sure you have the methyls

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:58.350
that are added and
subtracted from the DNA,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:00.570
that controls the DNA methylation clock.

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:03.050
If you have low levels of B12,

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:05.010
it's known that you have deficiency

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:07.320
in the ability to methylate DNA,

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.400
and that will mess up your epigenome,

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:11.760
and very likely accelerate the clock

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:13.330
in a way that causes aging.

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:14.550
And there are a lot of
studies that have shown

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:18.800
that deficiencies in B12
accelerate a variety of diseases,

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:20.870
heart disease is the major one,

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:23.450
but also dementia in the brain.

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:25.630
And the main reason I
think what's going on is

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:27.340
that aging is being accelerated

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:29.470
when you don't have enough
of these B vitamins.

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:32.030
- And the result of low B vitamins is

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.310
an elevated level of homocysteines.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:36.040
- Well, that's a markers, yeah, exactly.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:39.140
So often your doctor,
but not all the time,

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:40.490
measures homocysteine levels

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.340
because it's been shown to go up

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:46.090
as a predictor of heart
disease and dementia.

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:49.310
And high levels are
particularly dangerous.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.520
Most doctors would want you to have less

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:54.190
than 10 micromoles per liter.

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:55.810
Some people go as high as 100.

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:56.730
If you're that high,

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:57.760
you're certainly going to die

00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:00.940
in the next few years of
cardiovascular events.

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:03.270
So you try to keep the
homocysteine levels down.

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:04.970
And the best way to do that is

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:06.710
to make sure B12 levels are optimal,

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:08.890
not too high, 'cause that
can also cause problems.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:09.890
Again, you have to measure it.

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:12.460
I use InsideTracker to
measure my B vitamins.

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.170
But also you want to
avoid too much alcohol,

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:16.240
definitely don't smoke.

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.260
Your menopause will also affect
your homocysteine levels.

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:20.717
Certain types of cancer will.

00:29:20.717 --> 00:29:24.180
And aging itself leads to
increases in homocysteine.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.800
- And this all materializes as plaque.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:28.380
- Well it does, but homocysteine itself,

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:29.330
I don't think is the problem.

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.110
It's that the methylation pattern

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.720
on the DNA is getting messed up.

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:33.573
The sirtuins can't cope.

00:29:33.573 --> 00:29:36.410
SIRT1's not good at going
back to where it came from.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:38.000
And your body will get older.

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:39.870
And when your body is older,
it has more inflammation,

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:41.250
can't get rid of plaque,

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:42.660
builds up calcium in the arteries

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:44.390
instead of being in your bones,

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:45.810
and that we call diseases,

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:47.860
but that's actually aging going on.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.600
- I think a lot of people think
about elevated homocysteines

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:51.760
in terms of what's going on

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:54.150
in their blood vessels
throughout their body.

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:57.740
But we're having a conversation
here about our brains.

00:29:57.740 --> 00:30:00.950
It's important there as
well, maybe even more so.

00:30:00.950 --> 00:30:03.600
- Well, yeah, it's known that
high homocysteine levels,

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:06.030
which is a version of an amino acid,

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:09.680
do correlate with increased
susceptibility to dementia.

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:11.140
And the reason probably is

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.380
that we have vasculature in our brain,

00:30:13.380 --> 00:30:15.330
and that these are very small vessels

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:17.960
that are needed to bring
oxygen and take away toxins,

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:19.900
and they clog up really quickly.

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:22.760
And with high homocysteine
levels as an indicator,

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:24.110
we're getting plaque also building up.

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:26.550
And not only that, the endothelial cells

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:28.560
that are like the lining
of the blood vessels,

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:29.420
they literally are,

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.790
but they need to remain
flexible, like rubber bands,

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:35.620
they become defective, they
age, and they become stiff.

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:37.940
And then they start to
accumulate this cholesterol,

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:39.440
and then you get occlusions.

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:41.610
And that can ultimately end in a stroke.

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:43.200
But even before that,

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:45.060
those occlusions are limiting

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:46.710
the amount of oxygen your brain gets,

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:49.390
leading to of course losing your ability

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:51.260
to remember things, even at my age,

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:53.513
but ultimately what's
called vascular dementia.

00:30:53.513 --> 00:30:58.513
- There's a pretty simple way
to monitor that accumulation.

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:00.770
- Yeah, you just look in your eye.

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:01.710
Well, you don't look in your eye.

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:02.653
- It's really it hard.

00:31:02.653 --> 00:31:03.900
I actually tried earlier.

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:05.930
- Yeah, a doctor, an ophthalmologist,

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:07.950
or even an optician will look in there

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:10.120
and have a look at the back
of the eye, the retina,

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:11.660
and they can see very clearly.

00:31:11.660 --> 00:31:12.620
And they'll take photographs.

00:31:12.620 --> 00:31:14.340
They can show you of whether you've got

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:16.380
a perfect blood vessel lining,

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:18.250
these blood vessels sit
on top of the nerves,

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:21.900
which is a bad design, but
that's how we are designed.

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:23.380
They will see those blood vessels.

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:26.230
And if they're occluded, that's
a really, really bad sign.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:28.330
We've actually known for many decades

00:31:28.330 --> 00:31:31.320
that if you see one of these
occlusions and a lot of plaque,

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:32.820
you basically are going to suffer

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.620
from a heart attack and die pretty soon.

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:36.453
- Yeah, you have like a,

00:31:36.453 --> 00:31:39.030
it's like a 15% chance
of death within a year.

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:43.830
And then it goes up from
there in really scary measure.

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:44.663
- Exactly.

00:31:44.663 --> 00:31:48.280
In 1959, Dr. Robert Hollenhorst
found that these plaques,

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.451
these little occlusions that he could see

00:31:50.451 --> 00:31:54.640
in the back of the eye with
his lens predicted survival.

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:57.330
It got really bad, in
fact, what's now known,

00:31:57.330 --> 00:31:59.840
if you see these, you got a 50-50 chance

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:01.680
of being alive seven years later,

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:02.670
and that's really scary.

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:03.530
- That's really scary.

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.500
So obviously, staying ahead of this,

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:10.330
not letting that accumulate
at all is preferential,

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.940
even to catching it early.

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:13.900
- Right, and think of this.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:15.330
Your eyes are a window into your brain.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:16.850
In fact, your eyes are your brain.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:18.762
They're extensions of those
nerves with balls at the end-

00:32:18.762 --> 00:32:19.595
- I know, that's creepy.

00:32:19.595 --> 00:32:21.014
You told me that before,
and I got creeped out.

00:32:21.014 --> 00:32:21.847
- Yeah, when you look
at someone in the eye,

00:32:21.847 --> 00:32:23.470
you're actually looking at their brain.

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:24.303
Pretty cool.

00:32:24.303 --> 00:32:26.450
But what's great about is
you can see into your brain.

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:27.840
And that's what we're
talking about here is

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.130
getting an indicator
of how much occlusion,

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:32.930
plaque there is building
up inside your skull.

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:35.510
- Let's talk also about fatty acids

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:37.570
and the importance of really making sure

00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:38.587
that you're getting the right

00:32:38.587 --> 00:32:40.310
and the right kinds of fatty acids.

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:44.960
Particularly if you are
on a plant-based diet

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:46.440
and you're concerned about brain health,

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:48.420
which we all should be.

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:50.030
- Right, even with a normal diet,

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:52.680
you often don't get enough of
these omega three fatty acids,

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:55.870
which are the types that
we don't make ourselves.

00:32:55.870 --> 00:32:58.010
If you're only meat
and you don't eat fish,

00:32:58.010 --> 00:32:59.770
you eat animals besides fish,

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:01.410
you're not getting a lot of them.

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:03.140
And they're the building
blocks of the brain,

00:33:03.140 --> 00:33:04.010
so we need a lot of them.

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:05.480
And they've been shown
in a number of studies

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:07.420
to help with many different things,

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:10.210
from wound healing, and
of course, depression.

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:11.450
Now, what are the sources?

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:14.850
Well, if you eat fish, you're
probably in good shape.

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:18.090
You've got salmon and
mackerel, krill, sardines.

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:20.080
These are good sources of omega-3s.

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:22.170
- And these are giving
us the DHAs and the EPAs.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:24.210
'Cause there's three different
kinds of these, right?

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.690
- Well, there are lots,
but the three main ones

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:30.170
that people talk about are EPA and DHA.

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:31.890
The EPA is the more important one.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:33.810
You want to get at least a gram of that.

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:34.870
Sometimes people say,

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:38.680
get 1.6 grams of this ratio of EPA to DHA,

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:40.490
and women about 1.3 grams.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:41.323
And that's been shown

00:33:41.323 --> 00:33:44.960
to greatly improve memory
and counteract depression.

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:47.260
Now, if you're a plant-based person,

00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:49.410
you can't obviously get as much,

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.120
you have to focus on other types of food

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:54.060
that have what's called
alpha-linolenic acid or ALA,

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:56.340
which is converted
slowly, not efficiently.

00:33:56.340 --> 00:33:59.020
About 10% of it gets converted by the body

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:02.120
into the two types we just
mentioned that are important,

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:05.103
the DHA, and importantly, the EPA.

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:09.930
Focus then on flaxseed,
walnuts, chia seeds.

00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:11.920
That's where you get your ALA.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:14.260
Linseed oil is where it
was first discovered,

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:16.080
ALA, linolenic acid.

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.240
I use that to polish certain things,

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:20.920
keep wood looking good, cricket
bats, you put it on there.

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:22.700
But you can also, you
can consume a little bit.

00:34:22.700 --> 00:34:24.200
There's a lot of it in there as well.

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:25.890
And there's one other
thing I want to mention

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:27.750
that isn't in that list of three,

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:32.060
which is a monounsaturated
fatty acid called oleic acid,

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:33.220
which is really important.

00:34:33.220 --> 00:34:34.270
And I mentioned it earlier.

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:36.580
It's a component of
olive oil and avocados.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:38.950
And I have that included in
my supplement every night,

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:41.130
along with these other components.

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:43.880
- As a recent convert to seaweed salads,

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.571
I would be remiss not to note
that you can actually get,

00:34:46.571 --> 00:34:48.050
it's one of the few plant sources

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:51.220
you can get DHA and EPA
from is from seaweed.

00:34:51.220 --> 00:34:52.600
- Is that right?
- It is right.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:53.673
- Well, I don't know if
you get enough of it.

00:34:53.673 --> 00:34:56.340
I think it's really a
good thing to consider.

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:58.330
And talk to your doctor about it,

00:34:58.330 --> 00:34:59.650
to take at least a gram

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:02.010
of these omega-3 fatty acids every day.

00:35:02.010 --> 00:35:05.140
- So we know we should be
consuming these omega-3s, but why?

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:06.700
What are they doing on ourselves?

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:08.900
- So it turns out these
omega-3s actually form

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:10.540
a structural component of the brain.

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:13.900
They insert along with
other fats in the brain.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.460
So fat is actually good for the brain.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:17.950
A lot of our brain is
made up of these fats.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:20.450
The reason is that the
nerves aren't naked.

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:21.750
Much like an electrical wire,

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:23.760
you don't have them lying
around your house naked.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:24.850
They're actually wrapped

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:28.240
with insulation tape
or insulating material.

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:29.800
And that's what these fats do.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:31.920
And these are membranes that wrap around,

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:33.330
it's called the myelin sheath.

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:37.490
And these fats, actually,
some of them are omega-3s.

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:39.710
And the more omega-3s
you have in your diet,

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:41.090
the more you'll have in those membranes.

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:43.270
And that protects from
inflammation and damage,

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:44.720
and helps the nerves function

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:46.140
and repair if they get damaged.

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:49.710
- We have to eat for brain
health because what we eat,

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.530
what we consume helps make up

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:54.410
the parts of our brain that
keep our brain healthy.

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.760
We also have to exercise for brain health.

00:35:57.760 --> 00:35:59.399
We can't neglect that part.

00:35:59.399 --> 00:36:00.470
And we can't think that exercise

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.680
just affects us from
here down, it doesn't.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:05.250
- Well, that's actually
known in dozens of studies.

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:06.410
It's been shown that if you do

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:08.420
aerobic exercise or even just walk,

00:36:08.420 --> 00:36:10.050
that'll improve your chances of having

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:12.670
a better memory and
cognition as you get older.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:14.860
The reason we think that is,
is that there's two reasons.

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:16.560
One is better blood flow,

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:18.210
and also better neuronal activity

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:20.360
and slowing aging of those cells.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:21.600
That involves the sirtuins,

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:24.500
this third protective survival pathway.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:25.730
That can be activated, of course,

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:28.450
by the food, and also by exercise.

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:29.860
- We've seen this in a number

00:36:29.860 --> 00:36:32.070
of both human and animal studies.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:32.903
- Well, that's right.

00:36:32.903 --> 00:36:34.490
There are a number of studies
that we could talk about.

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:37.410
The one that stood out
for me in our research was

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:40.610
the one that involved 160
sedentary, sitting down,

00:36:40.610 --> 00:36:43.240
non-exercising adults that
were told for six months

00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:45.880
to do extra aerobic exercise,
to do some aerobic exercise.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:46.713
- Yeah, they were actually,

00:36:46.713 --> 00:36:48.310
they were led through this process,

00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:50.010
'cause you can't just tell
humans to do anything.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:51.330
They're just not going to do it.

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:52.163
- They had a cattle prod

00:36:52.163 --> 00:36:53.850
and they're pushed onto a treadmill.

00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:54.683
Something like that?

00:36:54.683 --> 00:36:57.510
- I don't think it's
that highly regulated,

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:59.730
but they do make sure they
actually do the exercises.

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:02.730
- So Blumenthal and his colleagues
found, what was it, 2019,

00:37:03.970 --> 00:37:06.830
that this greatly improved
executive function.

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:08.490
- Executive function.
- Yeah.

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:10.080
- That's the function that's like co-equal

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.360
to the judicial and legislative function?

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:14.930
- It's kind of like that, but
kind of different, which is,

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:16.960
so you know a lot of kids
don't have executive function.

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:17.870
They can't focus.

00:37:17.870 --> 00:37:19.530
They can't do tasks right.

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:20.700
That's what executive function is.

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:22.510
Concentration, focus, do some tasks.

00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:25.220
- And so just a little bit of exercise,

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:27.800
six months of exercise
improved for these people,

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:29.720
they're all over the age of 55,

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:34.720
improved dramatically their
ability to do these things.

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:35.633
- Well, they did.

00:37:35.633 --> 00:37:36.466
And I think that that's one

00:37:36.466 --> 00:37:37.780
of the main reasons for exercising.

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:40.127
You might want to do it not to
just because you feel better,

00:37:40.127 --> 00:37:41.960
but you will think better too.

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:44.580
- It's hard to take people

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:46.460
who have Alzheimer's, Parkinson's disease,

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:50.310
other cognitive problems
through things like that,

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:52.610
because it's just, at a certain point,

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.010
you lose the ability to
get them to respond to you.

00:37:55.010 --> 00:37:57.470
But there's also lots of
evidence in mice for this.

00:37:57.470 --> 00:38:00.570
There's a study this year
out of Brazil that showed

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:02.700
that exercise had a
really significant effect

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:05.950
on mouse models of
Alzheimer's and Parkinson's.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.430
- Right, so it's not just
aging that exercise works on.

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:09.263
What you're saying,

00:38:09.263 --> 00:38:12.800
and what's in the literature
is that diseases of aging,

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:14.300
and also including Parkinson's,

00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:17.160
which is age-related,
are benefited as well.

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:18.030
There's another study.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:20.220
It doesn't have to
include aerobic exercise.

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:22.130
There's one where there's
strength exercise.

00:38:22.130 --> 00:38:24.590
So if you don't like running,
pick up some weights,

00:38:24.590 --> 00:38:27.560
because what's been found
in this study, this is 2013,

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:30.960
Perera and colleagues found
that in an elderly cohort,

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:32.760
they had 451 people,

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:35.860
just 10 weeks of strength
training increased the level

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:40.280
of factors that grow new
brain cells, new nerves.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:41.780
This marker is called BDNF,

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:43.680
or brain-derived neurotrophic factor.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:45.820
And we use that as a way of indicating

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.020
the youthfulness of the brain

00:38:47.020 --> 00:38:48.910
and regrowth of new nerve cells.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:49.903
- And the takeaway here is

00:38:49.903 --> 00:38:52.600
that at a time in many people's lives

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:55.220
when they're becoming less active,

00:38:55.220 --> 00:38:57.080
it's actually more
important than ever before

00:38:57.080 --> 00:38:59.510
to become more active and to stay active.

00:38:59.510 --> 00:39:00.760
- Before it's too late.

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:03.650
It's very hard to get a
very elderly grandparent

00:39:03.650 --> 00:39:06.110
or parent to get on a
treadmill or lift weights.

00:39:06.110 --> 00:39:07.350
So what we want to do for ourselves

00:39:07.350 --> 00:39:08.900
and for our parents and grandparents is

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:11.810
to get them moving early
on before it's too late.

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:15.970
- Even if we're eating well,
even if we're exercising,

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:18.410
presumably, our ancestors
did these things too.

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:20.850
If they hadn't, we wouldn't be here.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.230
They faced these sorts of adversities

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:24.880
all throughout their lives.

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:27.810
And that's what we're trying to mimic

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.380
through our diets and our exercise.

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:32.660
It still might not be enough,

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:35.180
because we do live these
lives of incredible comfort,

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:37.500
and we eat a lot of food.

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:42.500
And so we might need a
little additional help

00:39:42.933 --> 00:39:45.620
when it comes to keeping
our brains healthy.

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:47.370
This is where supplementation comes in.

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:51.310
And one of the supplements we
want to talk about is metformin.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:52.400
A lot of people are going to say,

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:54.320
oh wait, that's an AMPK activator.

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:56.000
You guys said you were talking about SIRT.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:58.290
But we're actually kind
of talking about both.

00:39:58.290 --> 00:39:59.123
- Well, yeah,

00:39:59.123 --> 00:40:00.720
there's crosstalk between
these various defenses.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:04.600
The AMPK pathway talks to
the NAD sirtuin pathway.

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:05.870
When you take metformin,

00:40:05.870 --> 00:40:09.550
and you get this mitochondrial
hormesis, mitohormesis,

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:11.030
that will raise NAD levels.

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:14.980
It'll stimulate the production
of the enzyme that turns NMN,

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:19.140
a precursor of NAD, into NAD
itself and raise NAD levels,

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:21.100
and get the sirtuins active
as well in the brain.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:23.610
- Okay, and we know that metformin is good

00:40:23.610 --> 00:40:27.799
for brain health from
a variety of studies.

00:40:27.799 --> 00:40:30.320
One we want to start with
today is this fish study,

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:31.440
which I did not know

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:33.630
that they did cognitive studies on fish.

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.950
- Fish are actually used
quite often in labs.

00:40:35.950 --> 00:40:38.270
And most of them live about
two years, as long as a mouse,

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:41.050
which doesn't make them
that much more advantageous,

00:40:41.050 --> 00:40:42.660
but there are short-lived fish.

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:45.120
There's one called Nothobranchius furzeri

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:46.600
that lives only a few months.

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:49.200
There's one, in this study,
they used redtail notho,

00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:53.350
which is guentheri,
Nothobranchius guentheri,

00:40:53.350 --> 00:40:54.730
and it comes from Zambia.

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:55.980
It lives a bit longer.

00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:58.830
Its environment isn't as
harsh as the other one.

00:40:58.830 --> 00:41:01.980
And over that year, what it
does is it breeds very quickly.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:05.210
It lays eggs that become
encased in a shell.

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:06.290
The fish then dies,

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:09.240
and those eggs will survive
until the rains come again.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:10.770
But it's a great model for aging,

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:13.300
because it goes through its
lifecycle super quickly,

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:16.660
but it's built of the same
stuff that mice and we are.

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:19.480
- And how do you tell that a fish is

00:41:20.520 --> 00:41:24.030
having cognitive impairment
or cognitive success?

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:26.010
- So it sounds crazy
measuring memory in a fish,

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:27.580
but they do have good memories.

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:29.190
You can test it in a variety of ways.

00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:31.050
You can put food and a light,

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:32.160
and see if they remember

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:33.480
that the light is where the food is.

00:41:33.480 --> 00:41:35.890
They come to the light if
they have a good memory.

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:37.850
You can put them through
mazes, like a mouse,

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:39.500
they swim through if
they've got a good memory,

00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:41.210
and they know where the
food is at the other end.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:44.610
And the third one is where you
test their fear conditioning.

00:41:44.610 --> 00:41:46.930
It's reaction and memory of fear.

00:41:46.930 --> 00:41:48.420
And you shine a red light in the tank,

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:50.600
and then you get a stir
like you would a cocktail,

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:52.090
and swizzle it around, and they,

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:53.850
sure, if they remember that,

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:54.860
next time they see the light,

00:41:54.860 --> 00:41:57.250
they should run away, or
swim away, I should say.

00:41:57.250 --> 00:41:58.670
And so what they did was,

00:41:58.670 --> 00:41:59.890
in this study was they found

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:01.980
that by treating these
fish with metformin,

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.650
put a little bit of
metformin in their food,

00:42:04.650 --> 00:42:06.070
those fish were able to remember

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:08.147
those tasks much better as they got older.

00:42:08.147 --> 00:42:10.280
- And so the Ted lasso quote

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:13.060
about being a goldfish and
immediately forgetting things,

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:14.240
that's not true?

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:16.290
- No, fish have a really
good memory actually,

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:20.070
and they have brains that
are quite similar to mammals.

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:23.480
- So when you give these
fish the metformin,

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:26.620
what's happening cellularly?

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:27.690
- Molecularly, what's happening is

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:29.040
that the metformin molecule gets

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:31.280
inside the cell and into the mitochondria

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.720
where there's what's called
the electron transport chain.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:37.260
This is a series of bundles
of proteins, five in all,

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:39.830
that pass electrons between
them like a hot potato,

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:41.570
and generate chemical energy.

00:42:41.570 --> 00:42:43.460
Metformin disrupts that first step,

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:46.050
and in doing so releases free radicals.

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:48.010
Superoxide anion is one that goes off

00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:49.810
and damages parts of the cell.

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:52.500
And we call this damage mitohormesis,

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:53.700
which is a little bit of damage

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.610
makes actually the cells stronger.

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:57.110
One of the main things that happens is

00:42:57.110 --> 00:42:58.770
that the cells react and say,

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:00.090
oh my goodness, we don't
have enough energy,

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:02.220
make more mitochondria,
more battery packs,

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:03.150
more energy for the body.

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:04.810
That's always a good thing with aging

00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:06.290
and health and longevity.

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:08.640
The other thing that happens
is that this signal sends

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.600
a protein called GLUT4 to
the outside of the cell

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:15.240
in the membrane to suck more
sugar out of the blood stream,

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:17.210
and having lower blood
sugar protects the body

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:19.160
from this caramelization process

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:21.770
that also causes many diseases,

00:43:21.770 --> 00:43:23.990
including dementia, by
clogging up the arteries.

00:43:23.990 --> 00:43:27.890
- High blood sugar is not
good for brain activity.

00:43:27.890 --> 00:43:30.080
- No, it's not good for any tissue.

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:32.560
What happens is this glucose
that's in your bloodstream,

00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:33.393
if it gets too high,

00:43:33.393 --> 00:43:36.210
it actually binds to proteins, about 10%,

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:39.160
five to 10% can be covered
in this glucose molecule.

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:40.750
That's bad for their function.

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:42.420
It's often hard to remove,

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:43.830
and it leads to dysfunction

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.960
particularly of the cardiovascular system.

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:49.490
- We're not just seeing improvement

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:53.070
in cognitive function
with metformin and fish.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:54.500
There's been studies on mice.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:55.530
There's been studies on rats.

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:57.880
There's been studies on humans.

00:43:57.880 --> 00:43:59.560
- Yeah, there's a bunch.

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:00.890
Our researchers did a great job,

00:44:00.890 --> 00:44:04.600
and they're all fairly
recent, 2014 to 2019.

00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:06.780
The one that I want to bring up is

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:09.130
Koenig and colleagues in 2017,

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:09.970
a really good study.

00:44:09.970 --> 00:44:12.070
It's randomized,
placebo-controlled crossover.

00:44:12.070 --> 00:44:14.040
That's what you always look for.

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:17.060
Metformin improved, again,
this executive functioning,

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:18.870
the ability to focus,

00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:22.496
by treating 20 non-diabetic
normal subjects.

00:44:22.496 --> 00:44:25.170
And they had mild dementia
when they started,

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:26.100
and they improved,

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:27.800
which means that dementia is

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:29.620
somewhat reversible with this drug.

00:44:29.620 --> 00:44:30.930
It also means that if you're starting

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:33.963
to lose focus in your
job, daily activities,

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:36.860
either taking Metformin may help,

00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:39.090
or just keeping your glucose level steady,

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:40.920
which is what a lot of us do,

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:44.070
and we measure that daily
with monitors on our arms.

00:44:44.070 --> 00:44:46.110
That is also a way to stay focused.

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:49.420
- And that's a small, really
well controlled study.

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:52.850
There's also a really big,
less well controlled study,

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:56.240
but nonetheless really
impressive study from 2019.

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:59.300
It took a really huge
cohort of diabetic patients,

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:01.010
tens of thousands of them.

00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:06.010
Dementia incidence for the
metformin users, 55% lower.

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:08.170
- So actually, it was preventing

00:45:08.170 --> 00:45:10.250
the onset of dementia by what, 55%?

00:45:10.250 --> 00:45:12.070
- Right.
- That's a massive number.

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:13.100
When we talk about these numbers,

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:15.970
often it's a five to
10% decrease in disease.

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:17.243
55% is a massive number

00:45:17.243 --> 00:45:19.380
that we should definitely
pay attention to.

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:22.650
- There's been some
suggestion that metformin,

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:25.760
in addition to acting on AMPK,

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:30.410
and then the chain of
custody moves its way down

00:45:30.410 --> 00:45:33.260
and AMPK acts upon NAD,

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:37.900
metformin actually might
impact NAD directly as well.

00:45:37.900 --> 00:45:39.770
- Yeah, so that study
was super interesting

00:45:39.770 --> 00:45:41.200
from a few years ago that metformin

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:44.790
could directly interact
with, bind to the enzyme,

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:48.140
and make that Muppet, that
Pac-Man creature go faster,

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:50.410
in the same way that
resveratrol has been shown to.

00:45:50.410 --> 00:45:51.937
But that was only done in one study.

00:45:51.937 --> 00:45:53.140
And of course, in all science,

00:45:53.140 --> 00:45:54.160
we need to reproduce it,

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:56.010
and we're still waiting on those results.

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:57.400
In my lab, we've tried it.

00:45:57.400 --> 00:46:00.180
We haven't had perfect results
yet, but we're still trying.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:01.013
- In the meantime,

00:46:01.013 --> 00:46:04.780
there are more direct
ways to impact NAD levels.

00:46:04.780 --> 00:46:07.120
And that's through NAD boosters.

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:08.940
There's been lots of
animal research on this.

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:10.780
It elevates cognitive functioning.

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:14.590
It promotes recovery after
brain injury, all good stuff.

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:16.260
- Yeah, why would you
want to supplement NAD

00:46:16.260 --> 00:46:17.330
in the first place?

00:46:17.330 --> 00:46:18.163
Well, it's known,

00:46:18.163 --> 00:46:20.107
just like the rest of the
body that in the brain,

00:46:20.107 --> 00:46:22.990
NAD levels go down for a
couple of main reasons.

00:46:22.990 --> 00:46:26.000
One is that we don't make
as much that NAMPT enzyme

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:28.810
that's activated by
metformin and exercise,

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:30.620
goes down, so you don't make as much.

00:46:30.620 --> 00:46:32.860
But also it was shown by Jeffrey Milbrandt

00:46:32.860 --> 00:46:36.640
at Washington University in a
couple of high profile papers

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:38.410
just in the last few years
that there's an enzyme

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:39.670
that gets turned on in nerve cells

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:42.330
when they're damaged called SARM1.

00:46:42.330 --> 00:46:44.500
And it depletes the cell rapidly of NAD.

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:48.030
So what you've got is a decrease
in the production of NAD,

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:50.760
also with an increase in
the degradation of NAD.

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:53.000
So supplementation we think is important,

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:54.730
to not just get the youthful levels back,

00:46:54.730 --> 00:46:56.990
but go beyond that, to mimic exercise,

00:46:56.990 --> 00:46:58.880
mimicking a perfect diet,

00:46:58.880 --> 00:46:59.910
especially for the elderly

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:01.570
who cannot always do those things.

00:47:01.570 --> 00:47:04.100
- And we've got a long
history now of research

00:47:04.100 --> 00:47:05.910
going back almost 20 years

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.777
of NAD supplementation on brain health.

00:47:07.777 --> 00:47:10.330
There was a study in 2004

00:47:10.330 --> 00:47:14.597
that showed treatment with
NADH slowed Alzheimer's.

00:47:14.597 --> 00:47:16.957
And a lot of people hear about NAD+.

00:47:16.957 --> 00:47:19.570
NADH might be a little unfamiliar.

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:21.070
- NADH is basically NAD

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:23.630
with a hydrogen atom attached to it.

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:26.440
NAD+ has a positive charge
like the end of a battery.

00:47:26.440 --> 00:47:27.800
And then if you stick the hydrogen

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:30.750
onto the vitamin B3 part of the NAD,

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.270
then that's going to be called NADH.

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:34.000
And that's important in a cell,

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:36.850
'cause that's the other
major function of NAD.

00:47:36.850 --> 00:47:38.260
One is to turn on the sirtuins

00:47:38.260 --> 00:47:40.130
and DNA repair and all that good stuff.

00:47:40.130 --> 00:47:43.780
But it also is known as a
hydrogen carrier molecule

00:47:43.780 --> 00:47:47.110
that takes hydrogens and moves
it from one place to another.

00:47:47.110 --> 00:47:52.110
- So why would NADH work if
NAD+ is what's the standard?

00:47:55.210 --> 00:47:57.750
- Well, what I think is
going on here is that,

00:47:57.750 --> 00:48:01.140
so NAD activates sirtuins in
a test tube and in the cell.

00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:03.090
NADH actually has the opposite effect,

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:04.380
so you don't want high levels.

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:05.410
So what's probably happening is

00:48:05.410 --> 00:48:07.538
that NADH gets into the bloodstream,

00:48:07.538 --> 00:48:10.180
gets degraded into its various components,

00:48:10.180 --> 00:48:11.830
vitamin B3, there's a phosphate,

00:48:11.830 --> 00:48:15.710
there's a part of DNA called
a nucleotide, the A letter,

00:48:15.710 --> 00:48:17.910
and they get reassembled back into NAD+.

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:20.340
You're just giving the components

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:23.305
in a concentrated form by taking NADH.

00:48:23.305 --> 00:48:28.050
- Another more recent
result, a combination of NR,

00:48:28.050 --> 00:48:29.570
which is another kind of NAD booster

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:31.280
that we talked about a few episodes back,

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:36.280
and pterostilbene slowed
down the progression of ALS.

00:48:37.550 --> 00:48:39.770
- So NR is different from NMN.

00:48:39.770 --> 00:48:41.200
Let's go through that again.

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:43.280
When you want to make NAD,

00:48:43.280 --> 00:48:45.110
what the cell does is it takes vitamin B3,

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:48.410
or niacin, or nicotinamide,
turns it into NR.

00:48:48.410 --> 00:48:51.420
So the nicotinamide gets
now a sugar, ribose.

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:54.340
And then to make NMN, it puts a phosphate,

00:48:54.340 --> 00:48:56.090
which is phosphorus and oxygen.

00:48:56.090 --> 00:48:58.830
And then it combines that
together to form NAD.

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:00.178
So those are the various steps.

00:49:00.178 --> 00:49:04.000
Now, what NR is, it's a
couple of steps back from NAD.

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:05.340
And so when you take NR,

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:07.490
it's made into NMN, made into NAD.

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:10.350
But it's been shown in
humans by taking large doses,

00:49:10.350 --> 00:49:12.733
about a gram of NR, you'll make NMN,

00:49:12.733 --> 00:49:15.610
and you'll make higher levels of NAD,

00:49:15.610 --> 00:49:17.040
which is shown to be important

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:19.590
in this study in ALS patients.

00:49:19.590 --> 00:49:22.230
Those ALS patients
actually benefited greatly

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:23.650
from this supplementation.

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:26.780
The other component I forgot
to mention is pterostilbene.

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:29.650
And the pterostilbene
part of it is resveratrol.

00:49:29.650 --> 00:49:32.510
It's resveratrol with three
methyl chemicals on it.

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:34.310
It's essentially a way of delivering

00:49:34.310 --> 00:49:37.600
in a pill form resveratrol
plus an NAD booster.

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:40.730
- This was a pretty short-term study.

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:44.220
It showed immediate
returns for ALS patients

00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:47.590
when they took this combination
of NR and pterostilbene.

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:48.650
But Lenny Guarente,

00:49:48.650 --> 00:49:51.560
who's running the study
out of his lab at MIT,

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:53.970
your former mentor there,

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:54.940
we chatted with him this morning.

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:55.773
He said, actually,

00:49:55.773 --> 00:49:58.760
they're now looking at a year long study

00:49:58.760 --> 00:50:02.300
so that they can see if
these results continue on

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:05.200
for a longer term for
patients who are dealing

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:08.190
with a really, really
debilitating disease.

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:11.030
- For which there is no cure,
or even effective treatment.

00:50:11.030 --> 00:50:12.020
So this would be a big deal.

00:50:12.020 --> 00:50:16.970
And most of these trials fail
in the first stage, phase one.

00:50:16.970 --> 00:50:19.800
They're in phase two, so they
made it further than most,

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:22.610
but of course, it's a very
difficult disease, ALS,

00:50:22.610 --> 00:50:25.250
Stephen Hawking, it's just a
terrible, debilitating disease,

00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:27.710
very hard to treat, but
fingers crossed for this one.

00:50:27.710 --> 00:50:30.880
Hopefully those patients
will continue to do better.

00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:32.120
- Can we talk a little bit

00:50:32.120 --> 00:50:35.980
about this idea of increasing blood flow,

00:50:35.980 --> 00:50:39.210
and what NAD boosters do for blood flow?

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:41.070
One of the studies that
you were involved in

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:46.070
showed vascular improvements in mice

00:50:47.570 --> 00:50:49.520
that were put on these boosters.

00:50:49.520 --> 00:50:54.260
But vascular flow isn't just
important in our bodies.

00:50:54.260 --> 00:50:56.020
It's, well, frankly,

00:50:56.020 --> 00:50:58.060
it's more important in our brains, right?

00:50:58.060 --> 00:50:59.330
- Right, we forget about our brain

00:50:59.330 --> 00:51:01.230
needing blood flow and oxygenation,

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:02.270
'cause we don't really see it.

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:04.970
It's not part of our daily thoughts.

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:05.940
But it's just as important,

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:08.540
if not more important
than the rest of the body.

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:12.900
We found not only does
NMN and SIRT1 activity

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:15.170
maintain the youthfulness and ability

00:51:15.170 --> 00:51:19.270
to grow new blood vessels
in the mouse as muscle,

00:51:19.270 --> 00:51:21.150
but we collaborated with Zoltan Ungvari

00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:24.860
at the University of
Oklahoma to show that mice,

00:51:24.860 --> 00:51:25.760
even in their brains,

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:28.350
benefit from NMN by
building new blood vessels.

00:51:28.350 --> 00:51:31.110
And those elderly mice not
just had better blood flow,

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:33.940
but could think and
remember things better.

00:51:33.940 --> 00:51:34.851
- And we think

00:51:34.851 --> 00:51:37.993
that's because of the
improvement in vascular tissue.

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:40.190
- Well, almost undoubtedly,

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:41.840
that's what was going on in those mice,

00:51:41.840 --> 00:51:43.550
because we could block the effect.

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:46.420
We could actually downregulate
the SIRT1 specifically

00:51:46.420 --> 00:51:49.130
in those endothelial cells
that line blood vessels,

00:51:49.130 --> 00:51:51.090
and then the benefit of NMN went away.

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:53.540
- And this turns mice with older brains

00:51:53.540 --> 00:51:55.960
into mice with younger brains?

00:51:55.960 --> 00:51:57.120
- That's essentially what happens

00:51:57.120 --> 00:52:00.480
when mice and people get
their ability to learn again,

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:02.490
that's reversing an aspect of aging.

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:05.410
- So before we move off of the topic

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:07.520
of sirtuins in the brain,

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:10.830
it's not just blood vessel
that are being affected.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.380
It's not just the cerebellum.

00:52:12.380 --> 00:52:13.420
There are other parts of the brain

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:17.050
that are also really
well impacted by SIRT.

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:19.020
- There are a few things I
want to mention about SIRT1

00:52:19.020 --> 00:52:20.800
before we leave this topic.

00:52:20.800 --> 00:52:22.320
One is that overexpression,

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:24.370
turning up SIRT1 in all of the nerve cells

00:52:24.370 --> 00:52:27.080
in the brain extends a mouse's lifespan

00:52:27.080 --> 00:52:28.920
and protects them against these diseases

00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:30.760
that we induce in them,

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:34.130
ALS, Huntington's, and
Alzheimer's disease.

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:34.963
The other thing is,

00:52:34.963 --> 00:52:36.150
there are a couple of
regions that are of note.

00:52:36.150 --> 00:52:37.720
One is the hippocampus,

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:39.990
which secretes hormones
into the bloodstream,

00:52:39.990 --> 00:52:42.150
and that can actually
reduce blood glucose levels

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:43.810
by talking to the liver.

00:52:43.810 --> 00:52:45.690
The other area of the
brain is the hippocampus.

00:52:45.690 --> 00:52:48.580
This is the part of the brain
that consolidates memory.

00:52:48.580 --> 00:52:49.860
And it was found in old mice,

00:52:49.860 --> 00:52:53.000
if we activated that part of
the brain with resveratrol,

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:55.580
or activated SIRT1 by
putting in more copies

00:52:55.580 --> 00:52:57.300
of that gene and turning it on,

00:52:57.300 --> 00:52:59.770
those mice had better
memory even in old age.

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:01.720
So really what this
says is that SIRT1 plays

00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:05.180
an amazingly important
role in delaying aging,

00:53:05.180 --> 00:53:07.420
preventing diseases of aging in a mouse,

00:53:07.420 --> 00:53:08.920
and potentially even in a human.

00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:11.220
We'll see how those trials continue.

00:53:11.220 --> 00:53:13.080
But let's talk a little bit about sleep,

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:15.920
'cause the hypothalamus
controls the circadian rhythm,

00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:18.210
day and night rhythms of the body.

00:53:18.210 --> 00:53:19.330
- Yeah, let's talk about sleep,

00:53:19.330 --> 00:53:23.720
because quite frankly, if you don't sleep,

00:53:23.720 --> 00:53:25.244
you're in a lot of trouble.

00:53:25.244 --> 00:53:27.250
And you're going to age faster.

00:53:27.250 --> 00:53:29.450
There's lots of research on this.

00:53:29.450 --> 00:53:30.800
- Yeah, even at the molecular level,

00:53:30.800 --> 00:53:32.830
we understand that SIRT1 and NAD play

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.510
a fundamental role in controlling
your wake-sleep cycle.

00:53:35.510 --> 00:53:38.360
SIRT1 and NAD are going up in the morning,

00:53:38.360 --> 00:53:39.380
coming down later in the day,

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:42.060
getting your body ready
for sleep, and in doing so,

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:45.080
what they do is they turn on
a particular gene called BMAL,

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:46.460
which is part of the clock,

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:48.360
not the Horvath clock,
but the daily clock,

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:50.070
the circadian rhythm clock.

00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:53.370
And those genes tell
the liver to calm down,

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:55.120
it tells the brain to calm down,

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:57.480
and in the morning, tells
everything to wake up again.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:53:59.660
And so what is really
important to understand is

00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:01.210
if you start to lose the function

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:03.360
of SIRT1 and have low NAD levels,

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:05.310
you're probably not going to sleep well,

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:08.290
but also, you're going to age prematurely.

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:10.360
- And the big problem here is

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:12.960
that sleep efficiency
actually declines with age.

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:16.070
So we got to work harder
at sleep as we get older,

00:54:16.070 --> 00:54:17.990
just like we have to
work harder at exercise

00:54:17.990 --> 00:54:21.040
as we get older to promote brain health.

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:23.320
- Yeah, another way of saying
it is that as you get older,

00:54:23.320 --> 00:54:25.200
you lose your ability to sleep.

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:26.340
And if you don't sleep well,

00:54:26.340 --> 00:54:27.900
you'll lose your ability to fight aging.

00:54:27.900 --> 00:54:30.700
And it's just a feed forward disaster.

00:54:30.700 --> 00:54:31.790
So you've got to intervene.

00:54:31.790 --> 00:54:32.623
You can intervene

00:54:32.623 --> 00:54:33.920
with the kind of things
we talk about here,

00:54:33.920 --> 00:54:36.730
which is eating well, exercising,

00:54:36.730 --> 00:54:38.313
and intervening with the kind of things

00:54:38.313 --> 00:54:40.830
that you can take perhaps as a supplement.

00:54:40.830 --> 00:54:42.840
But now, let's talk about what do we do

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:44.590
to make sure we sleep well,

00:54:44.590 --> 00:54:46.350
and we have the right rhythm.

00:54:46.350 --> 00:54:48.840
And one of the key
things that I use is NMN.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:51.950
NMN is going to raise NAD
levels in the morning.

00:54:51.950 --> 00:54:53.730
I take a gram of it then.

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:57.140
But I also, when I travel,
I use it to reset my body.

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:00.980
And I definitely feel that I
can avoid jet lag if I do that.

00:55:00.980 --> 00:55:03.810
- There's some other supplements
that a lot of people take.

00:55:03.810 --> 00:55:04.643
Magnesium?

00:55:04.643 --> 00:55:05.476
- That's good for sleep.

00:55:05.476 --> 00:55:07.500
L-theanine is another one that people try.

00:55:07.500 --> 00:55:09.343
I've used it, seems to help me.

00:55:10.750 --> 00:55:12.770
But essentially, you just
want to calm down at night.

00:55:12.770 --> 00:55:14.550
Don't do your emails too late.

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:15.500
Relax your brain.

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:17.980
- And then I think a
little counterintuitive,

00:55:17.980 --> 00:55:21.410
one of the best things that you
can do for sleep at night is

00:55:21.410 --> 00:55:23.240
actually not something you
do before you go to sleep.

00:55:23.240 --> 00:55:25.640
It's something you do right
away when you wake up.

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:26.772
- You mean go outside?

00:55:26.772 --> 00:55:27.950
Get some light?
- Well, you got to get light.

00:55:27.950 --> 00:55:30.080
You got to reset your circadian rhythms.

00:55:30.080 --> 00:55:32.410
And the best way to do
that is put yourself

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:35.490
in a situation where your
body knows it's daytime.

00:55:35.490 --> 00:55:36.323
- Well, you can.

00:55:36.323 --> 00:55:38.310
But here in Boston where I live,

00:55:38.310 --> 00:55:39.740
there's not a lot of light in winter.

00:55:39.740 --> 00:55:41.550
So I actually have some blue light

00:55:41.550 --> 00:55:44.960
that I can shine in my eyes
to get my cortisol levels up,

00:55:44.960 --> 00:55:46.220
synthetic way, not naturally.

00:55:46.220 --> 00:55:47.240
But whatever you do,

00:55:47.240 --> 00:55:48.870
try to get some light
early in the morning,

00:55:48.870 --> 00:55:50.740
'cause that always gives
you an energy boost

00:55:50.740 --> 00:55:52.960
and helps you reset your circadian rhythms

00:55:52.960 --> 00:55:54.750
if they're not perfectly in sync.

00:55:54.750 --> 00:55:58.030
- And like the other things
we've talked about today,

00:55:58.030 --> 00:55:59.410
there's lots and lots of research.

00:55:59.410 --> 00:56:01.420
We're not just making this up.

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:03.810
Sleep is important, we know this.

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:04.930
- Well, yeah, even in flies.

00:56:04.930 --> 00:56:05.763
Flies sleep.

00:56:05.763 --> 00:56:07.020
It's a little known fact.

00:56:07.020 --> 00:56:08.610
But before we get to the humans,

00:56:08.610 --> 00:56:10.790
I want to, this is a really cute study.

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:13.050
It was a study that was
in 2020 in fruit flies.

00:56:13.050 --> 00:56:15.740
They found that if you
deprive flies from sleep,

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:17.930
they have a lot of oxidative
stress in their gut,

00:56:17.930 --> 00:56:20.440
and they also have a short
lifespan, which by the way,

00:56:20.440 --> 00:56:23.720
could be rescued by treating
them with an NAD booster.

00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:25.600
- Which is also a way that we've seen

00:56:25.600 --> 00:56:30.180
that you can rescue human
subjects from sleep deprivation.

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:33.410
But even just one night
of sleep deprivation,

00:56:33.410 --> 00:56:35.740
a lot of people say, oh, you
know, I'll catch up tomorrow.

00:56:35.740 --> 00:56:37.560
I'll catch up the next day, whatever.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:38.710
Maybe I didn't sleep well tonight,

00:56:38.710 --> 00:56:40.570
but I can sleep well the rest of the week.

00:56:40.570 --> 00:56:43.280
One night of sleep deprivation increases

00:56:43.280 --> 00:56:47.693
amyloid beta production by 5%.

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:52.020
That's, you don't want to
miss with amyloid beta, right?

00:56:52.020 --> 00:56:53.980
- No, that will accumulate in your brain.

00:56:53.980 --> 00:56:55.300
It's very hard to get rid of.

00:56:55.300 --> 00:56:56.780
And I was also shocked to read

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:00.310
that it's not just the brain
that ages if you don't sleep.

00:57:00.310 --> 00:57:03.150
We already know that if you
restrict rats from sleep,

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:06.120
they get diabetes within two weeks.

00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:07.720
In humans, looking at a million people,

00:57:07.720 --> 00:57:10.320
this study's from 2010, Capucho et al.

00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:11.153
What they found was

00:57:11.153 --> 00:57:13.740
that in people that had very little sleep,

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:16.460
the risk of dying was 30% higher

00:57:16.460 --> 00:57:18.960
than those that got a
natural, normal night's sleep.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:19.793
- And the thing is,

00:57:19.793 --> 00:57:23.350
our brains are getting so
much adversity right now.

00:57:23.350 --> 00:57:25.950
We talk about a little bit
of adversity being good,

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:28.430
but we evolved to have a pretty low,

00:57:28.430 --> 00:57:32.340
constant low level of adversity
popping up now and then.

00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:34.970
And right now in terms of the insults

00:57:34.970 --> 00:57:37.430
and injuries that we're taking
in in terms of stresses,

00:57:37.430 --> 00:57:40.450
daily stresses, everything changing,

00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:43.530
our brains are being
besieged all the time,

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:45.940
we need sleep to reset.

00:57:45.940 --> 00:57:46.773
- It's just too much.

00:57:46.773 --> 00:57:48.440
There's too much to remember.

00:57:48.440 --> 00:57:50.870
There's too much to cope
with, too much anxiety.

00:57:50.870 --> 00:57:52.500
We just are living through a pandemic.

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:54.230
This is really stressful times.

00:57:54.230 --> 00:57:56.130
And just lack of sleep makes it worse.

00:57:56.130 --> 00:57:59.980
And physically, we will
regret it decades later.

00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:02.300
- The overall message today is

00:58:02.300 --> 00:58:03.770
we got to keep our brains healthy.

00:58:03.770 --> 00:58:07.300
And we have to work to
keep our brains healthy.

00:58:07.300 --> 00:58:08.720
We can't just expect

00:58:08.720 --> 00:58:10.730
that they're going to do
what they've always done

00:58:10.730 --> 00:58:12.230
throughout our evolutionary history,

00:58:12.230 --> 00:58:15.030
which is to last longer than we have.

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:18.120
We've figured out many of the tricks

00:58:18.120 --> 00:58:21.900
that are going to keep us
alive for longer holistically,

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:24.230
but we got to keep our brains healthy

00:58:24.230 --> 00:58:28.150
for at least one day longer
than the rest of us last.

00:58:28.150 --> 00:58:30.520
- It's super important that
we look after our brains.

00:58:30.520 --> 00:58:31.760
It's not just about ourselves.

00:58:31.760 --> 00:58:33.320
It's about our families.

00:58:33.320 --> 00:58:34.940
Many families have had to take care

00:58:34.940 --> 00:58:36.960
of parents and grandparents
that have dementia.

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:38.730
This is not pleasant for anybody.

00:58:38.730 --> 00:58:41.180
And we have a responsibility to society,

00:58:41.180 --> 00:58:42.850
and particularly our family members,

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:44.810
to stay healthy for longer,

00:58:44.810 --> 00:58:47.600
particularly keeping our
brains younger for longer.

00:58:47.600 --> 00:58:48.990
- In our next episode,

00:58:48.990 --> 00:58:52.640
we're going to be talking
about a lot of the things

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:57.370
that we sort of hinted at
throughout the series so far

00:58:58.280 --> 00:59:01.010
in terms of what's coming next.

00:59:01.010 --> 00:59:02.910
Maybe what's coming next in a few years,

00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:04.610
maybe what's coming next in 10 years.

00:59:04.610 --> 00:59:08.090
And what if we can keep ourselves

00:59:08.090 --> 00:59:10.930
from aging too quickly over
the next couple of decades,

00:59:10.930 --> 00:59:13.790
might be a quarter century away

00:59:13.790 --> 00:59:16.670
and sort of waiting for us
there if we get there healthy.

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:18.140
- Well, it's a super exciting time.

00:59:18.140 --> 00:59:19.690
The reason we're doing this podcast now is

00:59:19.690 --> 00:59:23.000
to bring the audience,
the world along with us,

00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:24.348
and to experience these changes

00:59:24.348 --> 00:59:26.660
essentially in real time
as they're being made.

00:59:26.660 --> 00:59:28.930
And the kind of results
that are in the lab

00:59:28.930 --> 00:59:32.080
and increasingly going
to customers, consumers,

00:59:32.080 --> 00:59:35.370
and eventually to patients
in hospitals and at home

00:59:35.370 --> 00:59:37.370
with medicines will be directed

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:38.690
towards lengthening lifespan,

00:59:38.690 --> 00:59:41.000
not just by one or two
years, but by decades.

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:42.420
And I can see that coming.

00:59:42.420 --> 00:59:43.790
We're going to talk about
some of these things

00:59:43.790 --> 00:59:46.580
that'll include wearables, monitors,

00:59:46.580 --> 00:59:48.610
and even age reversal technologies

00:59:48.610 --> 00:59:51.330
that get that Horvath DNA
methylation clock to go back,

00:59:51.330 --> 00:59:54.560
not just a couple of years,
but potentially by decades.

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:56.470
- One of the things that I noticed,

00:59:56.470 --> 00:59:59.343
and we've talked about
this a lot, you and I,

